evis. Founded 1627. Population: 12,920. Active during the Early Modern period of exploration and nation-building. Classified under Cities.", "newValue": "Basseterre, established in 1627 by French colonists under Pierre Belain d'Esnambuc, stands as one of the oldest continuously inhabited European settlements in the Lesser Antilles and the enduring capital of Saint Kitts and Nevis. Strategically positioned on the southwestern coast of Saint Kitts, it rapidly evolved from a modest trading post into a pivotal port city, serving as a critical hub for both French and later British imperial ambitions in the Caribbean. Its significance during the Early Modern period stems from its role in the fierce competition for West Indian sugar wealth and its function as an administrative and military stronghold in a highly contested region.

Throughout its early history, Basseterre was repeatedly caught in the crossfire of Anglo-French colonial wars. Initially named 'Basse Terre' (Low Land) by the French, it served as the capital of the French colony of Saint-Christophe (Saint Kitts) until 1727, when the island was fully ceded to Britain under the Treaty of Utrecht (1713). The city suffered extensive damage from conflicts, natural disasters like hurricanes and earthquakes, and a devastating fire in 1867. Despite these setbacks, its deep-water harbor facilitated the export of sugar, molasses, and rum produced by enslaved African labor on the island's plantations, making it an integral component of the transatlantic economic system.

